  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 123 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nGOES Water Vapor satellite trends and 12Z upper air analysis this\nmorning depicted a couple of upper-level lows, one located near\nthe Louisiana Gulf Coast, and the other low centered over eastern\nNebraska. Regional WSR-88D radar trends showed stratiform rain\nencompassing most of the Mid-South late morning with temperatures\nranging from the upper 30s to lower 40s into this afternoon. The\nmain concern in today's forecast is the potential for wintry\nprecipitation tonight into Thursday morning.\n\nRain is expected to continue across the Mid-South throughout the\nafternoon as the upper-level low to the south slowly moves east\naway from the region and a secondary low drops into the Middle\nand Lower Mississippi Valleys tonight into Thursday. Short-term\nmodels have trended higher on QPF amounts over the past 24 hours\ninto Thursday. Forecast model soundings show a nearly saturated\ncolumn into Thursday morning with boundary layer temperatures\ncooling enough for rain to transition to a rain/snow mixture,\nthen to snow across areas near the MO/KY border later tonight\ninto Thanksgiving morning. The latest ECMWF EFI index and shift\nof tails favors a higher confidence for accumulating snow across\nthis region during the period. Confidence has increased enough to\nwarrant the issuance of a Winter Weather Advisory for portions of\nnortheast Arkansas, the Missouri Bootheel, and northwest\nTennessee for up to two inches, especially near the CWA border\nand lesser amounts to the south. A worst case scenario would\nsuggest snowfall amounts of 2-5 inches across this area with snow\namounts spreading southeast across much of West Tennessee. \nHowever, confidence remains low with the worst case scenario \nverifying but will continue to monitor trends in upcoming model \ndata sets. Temperatures will remain cold on Thanksgiving day with \nhighs only expected to reach the 40s.\n\nDrier air will gradually filter in Thursday evening as short-wave\nridging temporarily builds into the Mid-South for the start of\nthe upcoming weekend. Long term deterministic and ensemble model\nsolutions indicate another mid-level trough will move through the\nMississippi Valley for the latter half of the weekend, bringing a\nreturn of rain chances back to the area Saturday night into\nSunday.",
